    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii.jpg" alt="Jayavarman VII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JayavarmanVII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Suzan Black, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sukhothai lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-river">Yom River</a>, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya</a>, in the plain of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. In the twelfth and early thirteenth centuries it was an outpost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, whose laterite temples still stand in the city. As Khmer power weakened after the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>, Tai-speaking groups who had settled in the river valleys began to assert themselves. According to later inscriptions, two Tai chiefs, Pho Khun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-inthrathit">Bang Klang Hao</a> of Mueang Bang Yang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pha-mueang">Pho Khun Pha Mueang of Mueang Rat</a>, joined forces to expel the Khmer governor of Sukhothai. Bang Klang Hao was installed as ruler with the title Si Inthrathit, founding the line later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-ruang-dynasty">Phra Ruang dynasty</a>. The date is usually given as around 1238, though it is conventional rather than recorded, and some historians prefer a slightly later date in the 1240s. The early state was one of several Tai principalities in the region, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a>, Nan and the future Lan Na, and it controlled little beyond Sukhothai and the twin city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a> to the north. According to the inscription of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a>, Si Inthrathit's family fought neighbouring rulers to secure the new kingdom, and his elder son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-mueang">Ban Mueang</a> succeeded him before Ram Khamhaeng took the throne, probably around 1279.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai.jpg" alt="Si Satchanalai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Proposed_locations_of_ancient_kingdoms_in_lower_Mekong_Valley_in_the_7th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomson Walt, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sukhothai's economy was based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-river">Yom</a> valley, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and water channels; the earthen dyke known as the Saritphong, west of the city, stored water from the hills. The famous inscription attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> describes a land of plenty where "in the water there are fish, in the fields there is rice" and where the king levied no tolls on traders leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> or riding horses to market, an idealised picture that reflects royal propaganda as much as reality. Sukhothai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a> became centres of ceramic production, and from the fourteenth century the kilns of Si Satchanalai produced glazed stoneware, including green celadons known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawankhalok-ware">Sawankhalok ware</a>, which were exported across maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Religion shaped daily life and the townscape. The rulers adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, brought by monks via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a> in the south, and built monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-mahathat-sukhothai">Wat Mahathat</a> at the centre of Sukhothai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-si-chum">Wat Si Chum</a> with its giant seated Buddha. Sukhothai artists created a distinctive style of Buddha image, including the graceful walking Buddha. Older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and local spirit cults persisted alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and the inscriptions mention a guardian spirit of the hill south of the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng.jpg" alt="Ram Khamhaeng" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ram_Khamhaeng_the_Great_(I).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. …, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sukhothai's armies were raised from the free men of its towns and villages under the command of the king and his relatives, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> were the prestige weapon of rulers. Warfare consisted mostly of raids, sieges of walled towns and duels between leaders on elephant-back. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> inscription records that at the age of nineteen the future king fought Khun Sam Chon, ruler of Mueang Chot, on elephants and drove him from the field, earning his name, "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> the Bold". Sukhothai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a> were enclosed by earthen ramparts and moats, the walls of Sukhothai forming a rectangle about two kilometres by one and a half. Expansion under Ram Khamhaeng in the late thirteenth century relied less on conquest than on the submission of local rulers, who recognised his overlordship as long as he was strong. The kingdom's military position was shaped by wider events: the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> and the Mongol campaigns against Pagan created space for new Tai powers. After Ram Khamhaeng's death, Sukhothai lacked the strength to hold its dependencies. In the 1370s King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommaracha-i">Borommaracha I of Ayutthaya</a> campaigned repeatedly in the north, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamphaeng-phet">Chakangrao</a> (Kamphaeng Phet), and in 1378 Sukhothai's ruler submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/li-thai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-thai.jpg" alt="Li Thai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/li-thai">Li Thai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahathammaracha_I.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">พระราชวชิรเมธี รองเจ้าคณะจังหวัดกำแพงเพชร เจ้าอาวาสวัดพระบรมธาตุนครชุม, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sukhothai's kings presented themselves as paternal rulers, styled <em>pho khun</em>, "father lord". The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> inscription describes a bell hung at the palace gate that any subject could ring to bring a grievance to the king, and claims the king judged disputes fairly and protected inheritance. Whether or not this reflects practice, it became the founding image of Thai kingship. Government was personal rather than bureaucratic. The core of the kingdom, Sukhothai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a> and nearby towns, was ruled directly by the king and his close kin, while more distant <em>mueang</em> kept their own rulers who sent tribute and troops, a pattern often described as a mandala of overlapping loyalties. Royal power could therefore expand quickly under a forceful king and contract just as quickly under a weaker one. In the mid-fourteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-thai">Li Thai</a>, also known as Maha Thammaracha I, recast kingship in Buddhist terms. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traiphum-phra-ruang">Traiphum Phra Ruang</a></em>, a treatise on Buddhist cosmology attributed to him, presented the ideal king as a righteous ruler whose merit sustained the world. Li Thai invited a senior monk from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, promoted the ordination lineage of the forest-dwelling monks and spent time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, which later became the main seat of the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/phra-phuttha-chinnarat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phra-phuttha-chinnarat.jpg" alt="Phra Phuttha Chinnarat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/phra-phuttha-chinnarat">Phra Phuttha Chinnarat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Phra_Si_Rattana_Mahathat,_Phitsanulok_(I).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. …, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sukhothai's importance in later history is greater than its political power ever was. Modern Thai historiography, shaped from the nineteenth century by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a>, who found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> inscription in 1833, and by twentieth-century nationalist historians, made Sukhothai the first Thai kingdom and the model of benevolent, Buddhist rule. The inscription, dated to 1292, credits Ram Khamhaeng with inventing the Thai alphabet; whatever its origin, the script used at Sukhothai was ancestral to modern Thai writing. In the late 1980s scholars including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-vickery">Michael Vickery</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piriya-krairiksh">Piriya Krairiksh</a> questioned whether the inscription was a later composition, possibly of the nineteenth century, and the debate remains unresolved, though many specialists accept it as substantially of the period. Sukhothai's religious legacy is more secure: its patronage of Sri Lankan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> helped make that tradition dominant in the Thai kingdoms, and its Buddha images, especially the walking Buddha and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-phuttha-chinnarat">Phra Phuttha Chinnarat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, became models for later art. Its ceramics are found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Sukhothai's institutions and religious ideals passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, which absorbed it. The ruins of Sukhothai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamphaeng-phet">Kamphaeng Phet</a> were inscribed together as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1991.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i.jpg" alt="Ramathibodi I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i">Ramathibodi I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%B9%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%87342.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CKasa, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sukhothai's power depended on the personal authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a>, and after his death, around 1298, many of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> broke away. Under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loe-thai">Loe Thai</a> the kingdom shrank to its core around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yom-river">Yom</a> and Nan valleys, while new centres rose around it. The most dangerous was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, founded in 1351 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i">Uthong</a> in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya</a> basin, with access to the sea and the wealth of maritime trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-thai">Li Thai</a> restored some prestige and influence in the 1350s and 1360s, but after his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommaracha-i">Borommaracha I of Ayutthaya</a> attacked the northern towns, and in 1378 Sukhothai's ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammaracha-ii">Maha Thammaracha II</a>, submitted as a vassal. For the next sixty years the Phra Ruang kings ruled as subordinate princes, increasingly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, while Ayutthaya intervened in their affairs; in 1419 it settled a succession dispute between rival Sukhothai princes in favour of its own candidate. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammaracha-iv">Maha Thammaracha IV</a> died in 1438, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommarachathirat-ii">Borommaracha II of Ayutthaya</a> sent his son, the future King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat">Borommatrailokkanat</a>, to govern Phitsanulok, and the former kingdom became part of Ayutthaya's northern provinces. Its cities declined, and the region later became a battleground between Ayutthaya and Lan Na and, in the sixteenth century, the Burmese.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sukhothai was a modest Tai kingdom that rose as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> power waned, but its patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, its early Thai script and the idealised image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a>'s paternal rule made it the founding kingdom of Thai national history and a model for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>.</em></p>

